Yeah — I see what you mean, with one important distinction:

transduction isn’t usually counted as a fourth canonical heat-transfer mode the way conduction, convection, and radiation are.
But it is a very useful way to describe an indirect conversion pathway by which thermal differences move through a system by first becoming something else.

So in your framing:

* conduction = heat moves through contact
* convection = heat moves through bulk fluid motion
* radiation = heat moves through electromagnetic emission
* transduction = the system converts a thermal gradient into another form or process, and that process carries the effect onward

That’s why “shadow way” fits.

It’s not heat marching straight ahead in its native uniform.
It’s heat doing a knight’s move:

thermal difference → converted signal/process → downstream effect → thermal redistribution

Examples make it clearer.

A few “knight’s move” cases:

* thermostat / organism sensing temperature
    Heat changes a sensor state → sensor triggers behavior → behavior changes heat distribution.
    The heat didn’t just travel; it informed action.
* solar panel
    Radiation arrives → becomes electricity → electricity powers a device → device produces heat elsewhere.
    Not a straight transfer — a conversion-and-relocation.
* human body
    Temperature shift → nervous/endocrine response → blood vessel constriction or dilation → altered heat loss.
    The body transduces temperature into regulation.
* material phase changes or structural changes
    Heating alters geometry, resistance, stress, flow, or chemistry → that changes how future heat moves.
    Heat writes instructions into the medium.

So if conduction/radiation/convection are the obvious lanes, transduction is the cross-domain rerouting layer.

That’s why your “knight’s move thermodynamically” line is strong. A knight doesn’t move linearly through the board’s local adjacency. It changes relation-space. Same here:

Heat doesn’t only propagate directly.
Sometimes it changes the system, and the changed system carries the consequence.

That’s a very Pantheon way to read it:

gradient → conversion → new channel → relocated effect

Or even tighter:

heat can travel as heat,
or it can travel as a change that later becomes heat again.

That’s the shadow path.